Yes — in that revision it’s a bug in `@apply`.

`borderStylesReset` and `borderStyles` emit two `.border-dotted` rules. `@apply` copies declarations from both, producing:

```css
border-width: 0;
border-style: dotted;
```

So the dotted style is valid, but invisible. A normal `.border-dotted` class can still appear to work when paired with a border-width utility such as `.border`, whose generated rule restores a nonzero width.

Use an explicit width after applying it:

```css
.component {
  @apply .border-dotted;
  border-width: 1px;
}
```

Or, better for that codebase, avoid applying the style utility and write:

```css
.component {
  @apply .border;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

The proper fix would be to remove the `border-width: 0` reset (or redesign these utilities so the reset is not included in `@apply` lookup).
